Question: Which statement best integrates the excerpt’s two main ideas: the motivation from the coding-assistant/misconception examples and the claim about replacing RL-based preference learning with a simpler objective?
(A) Because high-quality coding behavior and correct beliefs may be relatively rare in the pretraining data, the excerpt argues that reinforcement learning is necessary to amplify them; a binary cross-entropy loss would mainly recover the average behavior found in human-generated data and therefore would not support alignment.
(B) The excerpt treats alignment as steering which responses the model produces from the knowledge and abilities it already has, not as erasing its awareness of mistakes or false beliefs; therefore, if the usual RL preference objective can be matched exactly by binary cross-entropy, the behavior-selection goal is preserved while the training pipeline is simplified.
(C) The examples are used to argue that alignment should remove undesirable knowledge from the model entirely, and the binary cross-entropy reformulation matters chiefly because classification-style objectives are better than RL at making the model forget programming mistakes and common misconceptions.
(D) The passage implies that safety, performance, and controllability mostly come from scaling unsupervised pretraining on large datasets, since larger models naturally separate good coding from bad coding and truth from misconception; the discussion of preference learning objectives is presented as a minor implementation detail.
(E) None of the above
Answer:","You will be given an excerpt from 'Direct Preference Optimization: Your Language Model is Secretly a Reward Model'. Read it carefully and answer the multiple-choice question that follows.

Excerpt:
Large unsupervised language models (LMs) trained on very large datasets acquire surprising capabilities~\citep{chowdhery2022palm, brown2020language, touvron2023llama,bubeck2023sparks}. However, these models are trained on data generated by humans with a wide variety of goals, priorities, and skillsets. Some of these goals and skillsets may not be desirable to imitate; for example, while we may want our AI coding assistant to \textit{understand} common programming mistakes in order to correct them, nevertheless, when generating code, we would like to bias our model toward the (potentially rare) high-quality coding ability present in its training data. Similarly, we might want our language model to be \textit{aware} of a common misconception believed by 50\% of people, but we certainly do not want the model to claim this misconception to be true in 50\% of queries about it! In other words, selecting the model's \emph{desired responses and behavior} from its very wide \textit{knowledge and abilities} is crucial to building AI systems that are safe, performant, and controllable \citep{ouyang2022training}. While existing methods typically steer LMs to match human preferences using reinforcement learning (RL), we will show that the RL-based objective used by existing methods can be optimized exactly with a simple binary cross-entropy objective, greatly simplifying the preference learning pipeline.

