Question: Which option most accurately characterizes how the SFT model is used during the RL phase and why the KL penalty is retained in the objective?
(A) The SFT model plays a dual role: it is both the reference policy in the KL term and the initial setting of the RL policy; the coefficient β governs how far optimization can move away from that anchor, which helps keep the policy in regions where reward estimates remain reliable and also reduces the risk of collapsing onto a single high-reward response.
(B) The SFT model is used only to initialize the policy, while the KL term measures deviation from the training data distribution and mainly serves to stabilize optimization of the reward model itself.
(C) Although the KL term is computed against the SFT model, the RL policy is deliberately initialized from a different model so the penalty can pull it back toward the reference; its main purpose is to improve expected reward, not to affect diversity or the validity of reward-model scores.
(D) The learned reward model serves as the reference policy for the KL term, while the SFT model is kept separate; β is introduced primarily to encourage broader exploration and thereby increase diversity, even if the policy drifts far from where the reward model is accurate.
