(B) It acts as a calibration term that directly improves the learned reward function itself, rather than constraining the policy to stay near the distribution where the reward model was trained.
(C) It keeps the policy close to the distribution where the learned reward model is accurate, and it also preserves diversity by preventing collapse onto a single high-reward response. So it protects both reward-model validity and output quality.
(D) It mainly stabilizes optimization by penalizing large policy updates, but it does not play a role in the reward model’s validity or in preserving response diversity.
(E) None of the above"
