Question: Which synthesis of the excerpt best explains why the authors position their method as neither a straightforward contextual dueling bandit solution nor a standard preference-based RL pipeline?
(A) The key mismatch is that contextual dueling bandits and standard preference-based RL both depend on online rankings of actions rather than binary preferences, whereas the authors instead assume absolute reward labels; this is why they avoid both reward modeling and policy optimization in favor of directly identifying a von Neumann winner from batch data.
(B) Contextual dueling bandits are analyzed around online preference feedback and a von Neumann winner defined by at least a 50% expected win rate against any policy, while standard preference-based RL typically assumes binary preferences from an unknown latent scoring function that is first estimated as a reward model and then optimized; by contrast, the authors target a fixed offline batch of preference pairs and optimize the policy directly in a single stage.
(C) Contextual dueling bandits differ only in their game-theoretic optimality notion, but otherwise share the same fixed-batch setup as the authors; standard preference-based RL likewise shares the direct policy-learning objective, except that it uses an explicit scoring function only for evaluation rather than for optimization.
(D) Contextual dueling bandits already match the authors’ offline preference setting, except that they additionally assume absolute rewards and therefore do not need a von Neumann winner; standard preference-based RL mainly differs because it cannot reuse off-policy data, so the authors adopt single-stage learning chiefly to enable off-policy preference reuse.
