 present a single stage policy learning approach that directly optimizes a policy to satisfy preferences.",False,"In the related-work discussion outside language, what two properties distinguish DPO’s target setting from contextual dueling bandits and standard preference-based RL, and how do those differences motivate DPO’s single-stage approach?
(A) Unlike contextual dueling bandits, DPO assumes a von Neumann winner can be identified from online action rankings rather than offline data. Unlike standard preference-based RL, it directly optimizes a latent scoring function without ever learning a policy, which motivates separating reward estimation from policy learning.
(B) Unlike contextual dueling bandits, DPO assumes online preference labels rather than a fixed offline batch of comparisons. Unlike standard preference-based RL, it replaces the need for a policy objective with an explicit latent scoring function, which motivates a two-stage approach of first estimating the reward model and then optimizing the policy.
