Question: Which choice best reconstructs the excerpt’s implied logic for reusing an existing preference dataset in DPO, given that the ideal data-collection pipeline would have sampled both candidate completions from the same reference policy later used in the loss?
(A) When the original SFT policy is unavailable, the best substitute is to maximize likelihood of the full preference pairs under a new reference model and then increase the DPO temperature parameter β to compensate for any remaining distribution shift between that model and the data source.
(B) Because DPO only needs a fixed model inside the loss, one should choose the highest-quality available language model as the reference policy; when the original SFT policy is missing, fitting that model on both preferred and dispreferred completions best preserves the pairwise structure of the dataset.
(C) Since public preference datasets already contain human labels, the reference model no longer needs to reflect the policy that produced the sampled completions; instead, it should be initialized from the trainable policy and then updated jointly with DPO so that the loss remains aligned with current model behavior.
(D) If the dataset came from an SFT policy, the most faithful reference model is that same SFT policy; if that policy is unavailable, a reasonable fallback is to train a surrogate reference model by maximizing likelihood on the preferred completions alone, since the goal is to reduce mismatch with the unknown policy that originally generated the comparisons.
(E) None of the above
Answer:","You will be given an excerpt from 'Direct Preference Optimization: Your Language Model is Secretly a Reward Model'. Read it carefully and answer the multiple-choice question that follows.

Excerpt:
The general DPO pipeline is as follows: 1) Sample completions $y_1, y_2 \sim \pi_\text{ref}(\cdot \mid x)$ for every prompt $x$, label with human preferences to construct the offline dataset of preferences $\mathcal{D} = \{x^{(i)}, y_w^{(i)}, y_l)^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N$ and 2) optimize the language model $\pi_\theta$ to minimize $\mathcal{L}_\text{DPO}$ for the given $\pi_\text{ref}$ and $\mathcal{D}$ and desired $\beta$. In practice, one would like to reuse preference datasets publicly available, rather than generating samples and gathering human preferences. Since the preference datasets are sampled using $\pi^\text{SFT}$, we initialize $\pi_\text{ref} = \pi^\text{SFT}$ whenever available. However, when $\pi^\text{SFT}$ is not available, we initialize $\pi_\text{ref}$ by maximizing likelihood of preferred completions ${(x, y_w)}$, that is, ${\pi_\text{ref} = \argmax_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{x, y_w \sim \mathcal{D}}\left[\log \pi(y_w \mid x)\right]}$. This procedure helps mitigate the distribution shift between the true reference distribution which is unavailable, and $\pi_\text{ref}$ used by DPO.

