Answer:","When training from an existing preference dataset, why do the authors care so much about how $\pi_\text{ref}$ is chosen?","The excerpt says the standard pipeline samples candidate completions from the reference policy, so when reusing public preference data sampled from an SFT model, the reference should be that SFT model if available. If it is not available, the fallback is to fit a reference model by maximum likelihood on preferred completions (x, y_w), specifically to mitigate distribution shift relative to the unavailable true reference distribution.","The general DPO pipeline is as follows: 1) Sample completions $y_1, y_2 \sim \pi_\text{ref}(\cdot \mid x)$ for every prompt $x$, label with human preferences to construct the offline dataset of preferences $\mathcal{D} = \{x^{(i)}, y_w^{(i)}, y_l)^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N$ and 2) optimize the language model $\pi_\theta$ to minimize $\mathcal{L}_\text{DPO}$ for the given $\pi_\text{ref}$ and $\mathcal{D}$ and desired $\beta$. In practice, one would like to reuse preference datasets publicly available, rather than generating samples and gathering human preferences. Since the preference datasets are sampled using $\pi^\text{SFT}$, we initialize $\pi_\text{ref} = \pi^\text{SFT}$ whenever available. However, when $\pi^\text{SFT}$ is not available, we initialize $\pi_\text{ref}$ by maximizing likelihood of preferred completions ${(x, y_w)}$, that is, ${\pi_\text{ref} = \argmax_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{x, y_w \sim \mathcal{D}}\left[\log \pi(y_w \mid x)\right]}$. This procedure helps mitigate the distribution shift between the true reference distribution which is unavailable, and $\pi_\text{ref}$ used by DPO.",False,"When training from an existing preference dataset, why do the authors care so much about how $\pi_\text{ref}$ is chosen?
(A) If the original SFT model is available, using it as $\pi_\text{ref}$ matches the distribution that produced the preference data. If it is unavailable, fitting $\pi_\text{ref}$ on preferred completions reduces distribution shift between the unknown true reference distribution and the reference model DPO actually uses.
(B) Because the main goal is to make $pi_	ext{ref}$ as strong as possible for generation quality, and maximizing likelihood on preferred completions simply gives the highest-performing starting policy for DPO.
(C) Because $pi_	ext{ref}$ is the model that directly provides the human preference labels, so choosing it correctly ensures the annotations remain unbiased and consistent across the dataset.
(D) Because DPO requires $pi_	ext{ref}$ to be trained on both the preferred and dispreferred completions, and matching the dataset source mainly helps the optimization remain numerically stable.
(E) None of the above"
"According to the excerpt, which statement most accurately explains why PPO-style RLHF can still be practically unstable even when it is optimizing the same underlying objective as prior work with a DPO-equivalent reward, and how that differs from DPO’s handling of the corresponding term?
(A) The main role of the normalization term is to strengthen KL regularization; without it, the solution shifts toward the reference model, so prior RLHF systems estimate the term as a deterministic constant from human completions, while DPO avoids the issue by removing KL regularization from the objective.
(B) The instability comes from omitting a soft value term of the current policy ␀␀␀pi_theta; doing so changes the optimum unless an exact critic restores it, so prior work uses human-completion baselines mainly to preserve the correct KL penalty, whereas DPO keeps the same baseline implicitly through its critic.
(C) The normalization matters only because PPO and prior DPO-equivalent methods are actually optimizing different objectives; actor-critic methods therefore need a learned baseline to mimic DPO’s supervised preference loss, whereas DPO has no counterpart to the soft value term described in the control-as-inference view.
(D) The problematic term is tied to the reference policy rather than the learned policy, so it can be interpreted as a soft value for ␀␀␀pi_ref; dropping it leaves the optimizer’s target unchanged but makes policy-gradient estimates higher variance, which prior approaches try to handle with either a learned value function or a human-completion baseline acting like a one-sample Monte Carlo estimate, while DPO’s reward reparameterization avoids needing such baselines at all.
(E) None of the above",(D),"Question: In the paper 'Attention Is All You Need', the authors replace recurrence and convolutions entirely with attention. What is the primary architectural motivation for this design choice?
