Question: According to the excerpt, which statement most accurately explains why PPO-style RLHF can still be practically unstable even when it is optimizing the same underlying objective as prior work with a DPO-equivalent reward, and how that differs from DPO’s handling of the corresponding term?
(A) The main role of the normalization term is to strengthen KL regularization; without it, the solution shifts toward the reference model, so prior RLHF systems estimate the term as a deterministic constant from human completions, while DPO avoids the issue by removing KL regularization from the objective.
(B) The instability comes from omitting a soft value term of the current policy ␀␀␀pi_theta; doing so changes the optimum unless an exact critic restores it, so prior work uses human-completion baselines mainly to preserve the correct KL penalty, whereas DPO keeps the same baseline implicitly through its critic.
(C) The normalization matters only because PPO and prior DPO-equivalent methods are actually optimizing different objectives; actor-critic methods therefore need a learned baseline to mimic DPO’s supervised preference loss, whereas DPO has no counterpart to the soft value term described in the control-as-inference view.
(D) The problematic term is tied to the reference policy rather than the learned policy, so it can be interpreted as a soft value for ␀␀␀pi_ref; dropping it leaves the optimizer’s target unchanged but makes policy-gradient estimates higher variance, which prior approaches try to handle with either a learned value function or a human-completion baseline acting like a one-sample Monte Carlo estimate, while DPO’s reward reparameterization avoids needing such baselines at all.
(E) None of the above
