Answer:",How do the equivalence-class lemmas support the claim that DPO’s reward reparameterization does not lose generality?,"The excerpt defines equivalence as differing by some function of \(x\) only, and the projection subtracts exactly such an \(x\)-only normalization term. The lemmas say rewards in the same class induce the same preference distribution and the same constrained-RL optimal policy. The theorem/proof sketch then shows a member of every such class can be written as \(\beta \log \frac{\pi_r}{\pi_\text{ref}}\), so the reparameterization does not reduce expressivity over reward classes or optimal policies.","DPO is able to bypass both fitting an explicit reward and performing RL to learn the policy using a single maximum likelihood objective. Note the optimization objective Eq. \ref{eq:main_eq} is equivalent to a Bradley-Terry model with a reward parameterization $r^*(x, y) = \beta \log\frac{\pi^*_\theta(y \mid x)}{\pi_\text{ref}(y \mid x)}$ and we optimize our parametric model $\pi_{\theta}$, equivalently to the reward model optimization in Eq. \ref{eq:reward_model} under the change of variables. In this section we will build the theory behind this reparameterization, show that it does not constrain the class of learned reward models, and allows for the exact recovery of the optimal policy. We begin with by defining an equivalence relation between reward functions. \begin{definition} We say that two reward functions $r(x, y)$ and $r'(x, y)$ are equivalent iff ${r(x, y)-r'(x, y) = f(x)}$ for some function $f$. \end{definition} It is easy to see that this is indeed an equivalence relation, which partitions the set of reward functions into classes. We can state the following two lemmas: \begin{lemma}\label{lemma:same_prefrence} Under the Plackett-Luce, and in particular the Bradley-Terry, preference framework, two reward functions from the same class induce the same preference distribution. \end{lemma} \begin{lemma}\label{lemma:same_policy} Two reward functions from the same equivalence class induce the same optimal policy under the constrained RL problem. \end{lemma} The proofs are straightforward and we defer them to Appendix \ref{app:lemma1}. The first lemma is a well-known under-specification issue with the Plackett-Luce family of models \cite{plackett1975analysis}. Due to this under-specification, we usually have to impose additional identifiability constraints to achieve any guarantees on the MLE estimates from Eq. \ref{eq:reward_model} \cite{bong2022generalized}. The second lemma states that all reward functions from the same class yield the same optimal policy, hence for our final objective, we are only interested in recovering an arbitrary reward function from the optimal class. We prove the following Theorem in Appendix~\ref{app:thm1}: \begin{theorem}\label{thm:main} Under mild assumptions, all reward classes consistent with the Plackett-Luce (and Bradley-Terry in particular) models can be represented with the reparameterization ${r(x, y) = \beta \log \frac{\pi(y\mid x)}{\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)}}$ for some model $\pi(y\mid x)$ and a given reference model $\pi_\text{ref}(y \mid x)$. \end{theorem} \begin{sproof} Consider any reward function $r(x, y)$, which induces a corresponding optimal model $\pi_r(y \mid x)$, specified by Eq. \ref{eq:op_policy}. We will show that a reward function from the equivalence class of $r$ can be represented using the reparameterization given above. We define the projection $f$ as \begin{equation} f(r; \pi_\text{ref}, \beta)(x, y) = r(x, y) - \beta\log\sum_{y}\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta}r(x, y)\right) \end{equation} The operator $f$ simply normalizes the reward function with the logarithm of the partition function of $\pi_r$. Since the added normalization term is only a function of the prefix $x$, $f(r; \pi_\text{ref}, \beta)(x, y) $ is a reward function in the equivalence class of $r(x, y)$. Finally, replacing $r$ with the RHS of Eq.~\ref{eq:main_eq} (which holds for any reward function), we have $f(r; \pi_\text{ref}, \beta)(x, y) = \beta \log \frac{\pi_r(y\mid x)}{\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)}$. That is, the projection $f$ produces a member of the equivalence class of $r$ with the desired form, and we do not lose any generality in our reward model from the proposed reparameterization.",True,"How do the equivalence-class lemmas support the claim that DPO’s reward reparameterization does not lose generality?
(A) Under Bradley-Terry/Plackett-Luce, adding any prompt-only function to a reward leaves the induced preference distribution unchanged, and such equivalent rewards also induce the same KL-constrained optimal policy. The theorem then shows every equivalence class has a representative of the form $\beta \log \frac{\pi(y\mid x)}{\pi_\text{ref}(y\mid x)}$, so DPO can optimize that form without losing expressivity over reward classes or optimal policies.
(B) The argument relies on extra identifiability constraints to pick a unique reward, and the reparameterization is only valid for that single identified reward rather than for every equivalence class of rewards.
(C) The lemmas imply that equivalent rewards give the same preference distribution, but they do not preserve the optimal policy under the KL-constrained RL problem, so the reparameterization could still exclude some optimal policies.
(D) They show that rewards are only equivalent up to an additive constant independent of both $x$ and $y$, so the reparameterization is just a convenient normalization rather than a representation of every reward class.
(E) None of the above"
"Which interpretation of the summarization results is most consistent with the excerpt when considering the experimental setup, temperature sweep, and comparison baselines together?
(A) Because DPO, PPO, and Preferred-FT all start from the same GPT-J SFT checkpoint, DPO’s edge cannot be explained by a different base model; and since DPO beats PPO at PPO’s own best temperature, exceeds the best-of-N baseline at its maximum, stays more stable as temperature increases, and was reported without meaningful tuning of β, the observed advantage looks substantive rather than a decoding artifact and may even be conservative.
(B) The fairest reading is that DPO’s apparent win is mostly an artifact of comparing preference-based methods with a supervised baseline: once all methods are sampled across temperatures, PPO catches up to DPO, Preferred-FT clearly improves over SFT, and the only remaining uncertainty is whether best-of-N would overtake both under the same human evaluation protocol.
(C) Since ROUGE is said to correlate poorly with human preferences, the automatic win-rate comparison against reference summaries should be treated as weaker than the head-to-head study; taken together with DPO’s untuned β, this means the excerpt does not really justify any claim that DPO outperforms PPO, only that DPO might become competitive after hyperparameter tuning.
(D) The results mainly show that deterministic decoding favors DPO: its ~61% win rate at temperature 0.0 slightly exceeds PPO’s best result, but PPO is the more temperature-robust method overall, while DPO’s advantage disappears at higher temperatures and remains below the strongest best-of-N setting.
(E) None of the above",(A),"Question: In the paper 'Attention Is All You Need', the authors replace recurrence and convolutions entirely with attention. What is the primary architectural motivation for this design choice?
