Question: Which interpretation of the summarization results is most consistent with the excerpt when considering the experimental setup, temperature sweep, and comparison baselines together?
(A) Because DPO, PPO, and Preferred-FT all start from the same GPT-J SFT checkpoint, DPO’s edge cannot be explained by a different base model; and since DPO beats PPO at PPO’s own best temperature, exceeds the best-of-N baseline at its maximum, stays more stable as temperature increases, and was reported without meaningful tuning of β, the observed advantage looks substantive rather than a decoding artifact and may even be conservative.
(B) The fairest reading is that DPO’s apparent win is mostly an artifact of comparing preference-based methods with a supervised baseline: once all methods are sampled across temperatures, PPO catches up to DPO, Preferred-FT clearly improves over SFT, and the only remaining uncertainty is whether best-of-N would overtake both under the same human evaluation protocol.
(C) Since ROUGE is said to correlate poorly with human preferences, the automatic win-rate comparison against reference summaries should be treated as weaker than the head-to-head study; taken together with DPO’s untuned β, this means the excerpt does not really justify any claim that DPO outperforms PPO, only that DPO might become competitive after hyperparameter tuning.
(D) The results mainly show that deterministic decoding favors DPO: its ~61% win rate at temperature 0.0 slightly exceeds PPO’s best result, but PPO is the more temperature-robust method overall, while DPO’s advantage disappears at higher temperatures and remains below the strongest best-of-N setting.
(E) None of the above
Answer:","You will be given an excerpt from 'Direct Preference Optimization: Your Language Model is Secretly a Reward Model'. Read it carefully and answer the multiple-choice question that follows.

Excerpt:
Next, we evaluate fine-tuning performance of DPO on summarization and single-turn dialogue. For summarization, automatic evaluation metrics such as ROUGE can be poorly correlated with human preferences~\citep{stiennon2022learning}, and prior work has found that fine-tuning LMs using PPO on human preferences to provide more effective summaries. We evaluate different methods by sampling completions on the test split of TL;DR summarization dataset, and computing the average win rate against reference completions in the test set. The completions for all methods are sampled at temperatures varying from 0.0 to 1.0, and the win rates are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:frontier-tldr-main} (right). DPO, PPO and Preferred-FT all fine-tune the same GPT-J SFT model\footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/CarperAI/openai_summarize_tldr_sft}}. We find that DPO has a win rate of approximately 61\% at a temperature of 0.0, exceeding the performance of PPO at ~57\% at its optimal sampling temperature of 0.0. DPO also achieves a higher maximum win rate compared to the best of $N$ baseline. We note that we did not meaningfully tune DPO's $\beta$ hyperparameter, so these results may underestimate DPO's potential. Moreover, we find DPO to be much more robust to the sampling temperature than PPO, the performance of which can degrade to that of the base GPT-J model at high temperatures. Preferred-FT does not improve significantly over the SFT model. We also compare DPO and PPO head-to-head in human evaluations in Section~\ref{sec:human-judgments}, where DPO samples at temperature 0.25 were preferred 58\% times over PPO samples at temperature 0.

