Question: Which reading of the out-of-distribution CNN/DailyMail experiment is most consistent with the excerpt’s setup and the conclusion the authors draw from it?
(A) The result is especially persuasive because both methods were re-tuned for CNN/DailyMail with domain-specific sampling temperatures and a newly designed news-evaluation prompt, yet DPO still won; this shows DPO’s advantage survives even after giving PPO no TL;DR-specific handicap.
(B) The finding suggests DPO generalizes better chiefly because it had access to more unlabeled prompts than PPO during TL;DR training, so its stronger CNN/DailyMail performance is best interpreted as a benefit of broader unsupervised exposure rather than robustness to distribution shift.
(C) The experiment mainly tests whether DPO is less brittle than PPO to small prompt edits in GPT-4 evaluation, since the only substantive change was swapping the words ""forum post"" for ""news article"" while keeping the same Reddit TL;DR examples and temperature settings.
(D) The comparison keeps the Reddit TL;DR-trained policies fixed when moving to CNN/DailyMail: it uses the sampling temperatures that were chosen on TL;DR, and it reuses the same GPT-4(C) evaluation scheme with only a minimal wording change from ""forum post"" to ""news article."" Under those conditions, DPO still beats PPO by a significant margin, which the authors treat as initial evidence that DPO can generalize about as well as PPO even though PPO had extra unlabeled TL;DR prompts during training.
